title: Jumping to an entry
tags: GUI
kind: guide
aliases: find-entry jump-to-entry
summary: In a list panel, f asks for a name in the command input and moves the cursor onto the row — selected, not opened.

In a panel that shows rows under a cursor,
<<key "cursor:find self select" f>>
asks for an entry in the command input and moves the cursor onto it. Where
[[Find in a panel]] highlights text and selects nothing, this selects a row and
highlights nothing: the panel's keys — open, enter, restore, trash — then act on
what was found.

It is one command, <<cmd "cursor:find">>, run on the rows of whichever panel has
the focus ([[Generic panel commands]]). What a row is depends on the panel:

|!Panel |!Finds |!A candidate reads |
|[[file-manager panel]] |an entry of the directory shown |its name, with a final `/` for a directory |
|[[treeref panel]] |a child of the current node |its name |
|[[log panel]] |a write listed |`#12`, followed by its label when it has one |
|[[trash panel]] |an entry of the trash |its name, then the path it was trashed from |
|[[recent panel]] |a metarecord seen recently |its name, then its path |
|[[duplicates panel]] |a group or a copy |the group's hash and number of copies; the copy's path |

They all behave the same way:

* The line starts empty and completes over the rows the panel shows ''now'', narrowed by the pieces typed, in order ([[Interactive command arguments]]).
* What is typed is matched against the whole candidate. Where two rows can have the same name — the trash, the recent list — the path that tells them apart can therefore be typed too.
* Choosing a candidate selects that row. A text that is not a candidate (<<key "pick:confirm" Ctrl+Enter>>, or pieces matching several rows) selects the first row it matches, which is the first one the list was showing.
* A text matching nothing leaves the cursor where it was, and the status bar says `no entry matching "…"`. An empty answer does nothing.
* The row is ''selected, not opened'': a directory is not entered, a node is not descended into, a trashed file is not restored.
* Only the rows the panel has loaded are looked at.

!! Walking down a path

The second argument is the mode: `select` is the find above, and `walk`
(<<key "cursor:find self walk" d>>, for ''descend'') goes ''into'' what it finds.
A directory found — a node with children — is entered and the same question asked
again from inside, so a path is walked down one name at a time; a file found — a
leaf — is selected, and the walk stops. Whether a node has children is not known
beforehand, so the [[treeref panel]] asks the daemon for one before going in. To
stop on a directory, selected rather than entered, answer with `shift+Enter` — the
alternate answer of [[Interactive command arguments]], which runs the step as
`cursor:find self select`. `Escape` stops where the walk is.

Only the panels whose rows can be entered — the file manager and the tree —
provide what a walk needs (`descend`) — and the log, whose labels hold labels;
elsewhere the key passes through. Another panel gains the mode the day it
provides `descend`.

`metarecord-list` provides no entries. Its rows come from a query, not from a list
already loaded, so the same key (<<key "metarecord-list:clear finder" f>>) edits its
finder: the same question, asked of the daemon.
